WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.015
Alerts should point to conditions that require action, not every interesting fluctuation.

00:00:05.015 --> 00:00:06.120
In AI environments,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.191
noisy alerts can be especially tempting because there are many signals: latency,

00:00:10.191 --> 00:00:14.320
queue depth, GPU pressure, pipeline freshness, retrieval health, model-serving errors,

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:16.580
dependency failures, and policy decisions.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:20.990
Alert design should prioritize user impact, operational risk, and clear ownership.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.300
Service-level objectives help teams define what good behavior means.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.828
For AI systems, SLOs may cover inference latency, availability, error rates,

00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:32.580
timeout rates, queue delay, retrieval availability, pipeline freshness,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.595
model-serving success rates, and data processing reliability.

00:00:35.595 --> 00:00:40.270
These objectives turn vague concerns into measurable expectations that engineering and

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:41.990
operations teams can discuss together.

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:43.724
Runbooks connect alerts to action.

00:00:43.724 --> 00:00:48.710
A useful runbook points responders toward likely causes, dashboards, logs, traces, owners,

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:51.195
escalation paths, and safe remediation steps.

00:00:51.195 --> 00:00:54.760
It should explain how to distinguish a model-serving issue from scheduler pressure,

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:59.180
stale retrieval data, network delay, storage bottlenecks, or an external dependency.

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:02.630
Runbooks should also clarify what not to do when the evidence is incomplete.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:06.265
Incident-ready telemetry preserves evidence about impact, timeline, scope,

00:01:06.265 --> 00:01:07.655
and contributing factors.

00:01:07.655 --> 00:01:11.730
After an incident, the review should improve telemetry, not just fix the immediate issue.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:15.380
If responders lacked a label, dashboard, audit record, freshness signal,

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:17.780
or correlation ID, that gap is part of the learning.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:21.420
Better telemetry makes the next incident shorter, clearer,

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:22.880
and less dependent on individual memory.
